WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:07.620
- Good afternoon, ladies and gentlemen,

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:11.090
and welcome to the 2021 Yale University,

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:13.460
Joint Military Commissioning Ceremony.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:15.950
We are gathered here today
to continue a tradition

00:00:15.950 --> 00:00:18.660
rooted in military history and custom.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.410
Today, we celebrate
these young men and women

00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:24.100
joining the ranks of
those in our armed forces,

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.940
who have commissioned before them.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.640
We would like to thank all
of you gathered here today

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:30.940
for supporting these new officers.

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:33.470
And joining us for this
momentous occasion.

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:35.640
There are several moments in this ceremony

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:37.380
when it is appropriate to stand:

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:39.430
for the arrival of the official party,

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:40.660
the National Anthem,

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.040
the invocation,

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:44.140
and the departure of the official party.

00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:47.810
We ask that you now silence
all electronic devices.

00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:49.453
The ceremony will begin shortly.

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:54.410
Ladies and gentlemen,

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:57.020
please rise for the arrival
of the official party

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:00.253
and remain standing for the
National Anthem and invocation.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.990
Colonel George. R. Grandholm.

00:01:04.990 --> 00:01:07.480
United States Air Force commander,

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:10.743
Air Force ROTC detachment
nine, Yale University.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.780
Captain Ronald. L. Withrow.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:19.280
United States Navy,

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:20.540
commanding officer,

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:23.133
Naval ROTC, Yale University.

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:27.640
Today's honored guests
and keynote speaker.

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:28.860
President Salovey.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:31.712
President of Yale University.

00:01:31.712 --> 00:01:34.712
(audience clapping)

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.550
Scott A. Strobel.

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:41.985
Provost of Yale University.

00:01:41.985 --> 00:01:44.985
(audience clapping)

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.360
Kimberly M. Goff-Crews.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.180
Secretary and Vice
President for Student Life,

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:52.753
Yale University.

00:01:52.753 --> 00:01:55.753
(audience clapping)

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:00.479
Marvin Chun, Dean of Yale College.

00:02:00.479 --> 00:02:03.479
(audience clapping)

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:07.960
Sharon M. K. Kugler,

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:10.230
Yale University chaplain.

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:13.230
(audience clapping)

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:37.043
Oh,

00:02:37.043 --> 00:02:37.876
say

00:02:37.876 --> 00:02:41.050
can you see,

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:43.733
by the dawn's early light.

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:49.263
What so proudly we hailed,

00:02:50.484 --> 00:02:53.070
at the twilight's

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:53.903
last

00:02:53.903 --> 00:02:55.540
gleaming.

00:02:55.540 --> 00:03:00.470
Whose broad stripes and bright stars,

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:02.573
through the perilous fight,

00:03:05.075 --> 00:03:07.190
O'er the ramparts we watched,

00:03:09.491 --> 00:03:14.491
were so gallantly streaming?

00:03:14.496 --> 00:03:17.737
And the rocket's red glare,

00:03:18.749 --> 00:03:21.337
the bombs bursting in air,

00:03:23.029 --> 00:03:27.850
gave proof through the night,

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:31.443
that our flag was still there,

00:03:32.673 --> 00:03:33.620
Oh,

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:34.480
say

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:35.314
does

00:03:35.314 --> 00:03:36.205
that

00:03:36.205 --> 00:03:38.600
star-spangled,

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:40.230
banner

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:44.139
yet wave,

00:03:44.139 --> 00:03:45.222
O'er the land

00:03:46.442 --> 00:03:47.359
of the free

00:03:50.225 --> 00:03:51.225
and the home

00:03:53.532 --> 00:03:54.365
of the

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:56.573
brave.

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:14.023
Chaplain Kugler will now
deliver the invocation.

00:04:27.610 --> 00:04:29.960
- [Sharon Kugler] All
mighty, and gracious god.

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:34.053
It is you who are all powerful and loving.

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:38.103
It is you who calls us
to our full humanity.

00:04:38.943 --> 00:04:43.270
Today, may we feel thoroughly
embraced by your presence.

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:47.053
As we accept a new and
daunting responsibility.

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:50.040
We understand this commissioning

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:52.203
as both blessing and burden.

00:04:53.090 --> 00:04:57.270
Walk with us, as we seek to
be light in the darkness.

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:00.833
Striving to be generous,
merciful and just.

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:05.290
Enable us to act morally
and with boldness.

00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:09.510
To defend and foster,
the equality of a person,

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:13.523
the hopes of a people,
the justice of the ages.

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:18.710
Merciful God, as we begin our
lives as leaders in this time

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:21.060
fraught with uncertainty.

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:22.940
Bestow upon us,

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:26.460
the spirit of wise judgment and courage.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:29.730
The spirit of good counsel and strength.

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:34.000
The spirit of patient
knowledge and reverence.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:37.980
Eternal God, may our greatest
triumphs always be discovered,

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:40.620
as we walk in your light.

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:44.470
As your beloved children
from many families of faith.

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:48.390
May we be ever inspired,
by the spirit of service,

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:50.320
we witness on this day,

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:53.320
and all days, for this we say,

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:54.153
amen.

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:02.823
- Please be seated.

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:09.300
At this time,

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:10.190
I'd like to welcome,

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:11.190
Captain Withrow,

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:12.440
commanding officer

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:15.193
of Naval ROTC at Yale University.

00:06:16.529 --> 00:06:19.529
(audience clapping)

00:06:27.450 --> 00:06:28.800
- Good afternoon, everyone.

00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:39.580
Thank you, chaplain Kugler,

00:06:39.580 --> 00:06:41.910
for your kind and thought
provoking invocation.

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:43.110
You certainly set the stage,

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:45.660
for what is sure to be a dignified event.

00:06:45.660 --> 00:06:48.150
And wow, it certainly is
a beautiful day outside.

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:49.760
Isn't it?

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:52.070
It's great that there's a
little bit of a breeze here.

00:06:52.070 --> 00:06:53.660
Welcome everyone, both in person

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:55.740
and in simulcasts land out there.

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:57.660
A special welcome to president Salovey,

00:06:57.660 --> 00:06:58.493
Provost Strobel,

00:06:58.493 --> 00:06:59.670
Dean Chun.

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:02.340
Secretary and Vice President Goff-Crews.

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:04.550
Thank you for allowing us to
host this in person event.

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:05.850
We really appreciate this.

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:09.840
Also, welcome to the deans
and the heads of college.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:12.850
Also Dean Lavesque and Dean Sodi as well.

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:14.767
And also thank you, to those,

00:07:14.767 --> 00:07:18.220
for your incredible support
to our ROTC programs,

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:20.030
over the course of the past
year, and the many years

00:07:20.030 --> 00:07:21.260
previous.

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:23.310
I'd also like to ascend a welcome

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:26.690
to the members of the
Yale veterans association.

00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:28.660
Thank you for all that you
have done for our midshipmen

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:30.340
and cadets as well.

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:32.190
To the staffs of the Naval and Air Force

00:07:32.190 --> 00:07:34.740
ROTC, commissioning marks,

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:36.790
the culmination of years of your hard work

00:07:36.790 --> 00:07:39.530
fulfilling our mission to train
and develop these midshipmen

00:07:39.530 --> 00:07:41.820
and cadets, into military officers.

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:43.337
So well done.

00:07:43.337 --> 00:07:46.337
(audience clapping)

00:07:48.950 --> 00:07:51.740
I also wanted to say, welcome.

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:53.200
To retired rear Admiral Joe Callow

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:56.180
and his wife retired Captain
Sally Mackelreef Callow.

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:58.480
They're watching from
afar in New York city.

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:02.650
Admiral Callow is Yale
Naval ROTC class of 1958.

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:05.500
He's also a benefactor to Naval ROTC unit.

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:07.410
In fact, they're gracious endowment,

00:08:07.410 --> 00:08:09.710
in small part was used to
purchase commissioning kits

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:11.310
for each of the midshipmen,

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:12.754
definitely alleviating some of the,

00:08:12.754 --> 00:08:16.200
the financial burden of
initial uniform outlays.

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:18.800
So thank you, Admiral and Sally.

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:20.810
And saving the best for last.

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:22.870
A special welcome, to the
parents and loved ones

00:08:22.870 --> 00:08:24.680
of our midshipmen and
cadets, who are watching

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:25.601
from afar,

00:08:25.601 --> 00:08:28.040
maybe from across the Plaza
back there, I believe.

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:29.603
So another round of applause.

00:08:29.603 --> 00:08:32.603
(audience clapping)

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:36.780
So, what do I say,

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:39.560
to an intrepid group of
incredibly talented and spirited

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:41.738
men and women about to
embark on their careers

00:08:41.738 --> 00:08:44.440
in our nations Marine
Corps, Navy, Air Force

00:08:44.440 --> 00:08:45.910
and Space Force.

00:08:45.910 --> 00:08:47.240
Simply this.

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:49.263
You are ready, now get to work.

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:52.040
Now, this is not the end of my remarks.

00:08:52.040 --> 00:08:53.740
So we've got a couple of pages to go here.

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:55.660
So of course, I'm going to expound a bit.

00:08:55.660 --> 00:08:56.493
Your entire life,

00:08:56.493 --> 00:08:58.250
you have been developing
your personal virtues

00:08:58.250 --> 00:08:59.510
and leadership traits.

00:08:59.510 --> 00:09:03.620
You have your parents,
coaches, teachers, ROTC staff,

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:06.470
religious leaders, amongst
others to thank for that.

00:09:06.470 --> 00:09:09.500
But you should also enjoy
personal satisfaction.

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:11.870
Knowing your perseverance,
creativity, thirst

00:09:11.870 --> 00:09:13.750
for knowledge, are also
significant factors

00:09:13.750 --> 00:09:15.290
in you sitting here today.

00:09:15.290 --> 00:09:17.540
It's taken a lot of hard
work to get to this point.

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:20.070
The starting point of
your military careers.

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:22.300
And it will take a lot more
hard work on your part.

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:24.750
And on the part of the
teams you will lead.

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:25.583
And midshipman,

00:09:25.583 --> 00:09:28.090
I checked the latest revision
to U S Navy regulations,

00:09:28.090 --> 00:09:29.280
Before I came here.

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:31.100
I didn't see any prohibition
against hard work.

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:32.750
So hard work is still authorized.

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:36.810
Hard work is authorized,
but so is smart work.

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:37.980
You're all well aware of this,

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:41.060
after having navigated the past year plus.

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:42.990
We live in an era of revision as powers

00:09:42.990 --> 00:09:45.150
challenging our role
as a democratic leader,

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:47.760
and defender of freedom and
democracy around the world,

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:49.530
challenging us security
interests, and those

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:52.670
of our allies, and seeking
to consume weaker nations.

00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:55.110
This requires our military
to be strong and smart.

00:09:55.110 --> 00:09:56.393
You must be strong.

00:09:57.670 --> 00:09:59.974
In 1630, John Winthrop, leading Puritan,

00:09:59.974 --> 00:10:01.920
figure and first governor of the

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:03.100
Massachusetts bay colony,

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:05.170
spoke of Massachusetts
and his early settlers,

00:10:05.170 --> 00:10:06.770
as a city on the hill.

00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:08.630
A shining example to the world.

00:10:08.630 --> 00:10:11.610
His Clarion calls, continued
to define our nation.

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:13.280
But ours is a tenuous position,

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:14.630
requiring the resolve and commitment

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:16.960
of our people, our elected officials,

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:19.320
and our nation's military,
when called upon,

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:21.165
to deter those who wish ill upon us,

00:10:21.165 --> 00:10:23.560
our allies, and our shared interests.

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:25.360
And when necessary to confront them.

00:10:26.260 --> 00:10:28.210
You midshipman have learned,
of the critical role

00:10:28.210 --> 00:10:30.030
sea power, and maritime strategy plays

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:31.840
in the defense of our city on the hill,

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:32.673
our nation,

00:10:32.673 --> 00:10:33.740
the ideals we hold dear,

00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:34.990
and our allies.

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:36.410
You have been introduced
to the critical role

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:37.630
of joint teamwork,

00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:38.750
with our fellow services,

00:10:38.750 --> 00:10:40.720
such as the air force and space force.

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:42.580
And with our partner nations.

00:10:42.580 --> 00:10:44.810
Sea power requires people power,

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:46.250
and strong leadership,

00:10:46.250 --> 00:10:47.520
your leadership.

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:49.940
So today in about 30 minutes,
you'll recite the oath

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:52.700
of office, swearing loyalty
to our constitution,

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:54.750
and the country whose course it directs.

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:56.760
Then donning the cloth of our nation.

00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:58.240
And with sea bag in hand,

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:00.380
you will embark on the
journey of a lifetime,

00:11:00.380 --> 00:11:02.050
as a Naval officer.

00:11:02.050 --> 00:11:04.000
I rest easy knowing you
are taking the helm,

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:05.490
because I know you are ready.

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:07.640
And because I know you
will continue to work hard,

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:09.180
and smartly.

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:10.320
To our midshipman.

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:11.760
And this is the last
time that I will refer

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:13.580
to you as midshipman.

00:11:13.580 --> 00:11:16.260
I know I speak on behalf
of the staff of Naval ROTC,

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:19.257
when I say I'm incredibly
proud of your achievements,

00:11:19.257 --> 00:11:21.830
and greatly look forward
to watching, from a shore,

00:11:21.830 --> 00:11:23.980
as you cast off all
lines and get underway.

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:26.760
You are an extremely
talented and promising group

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:27.750
of leaders,

00:11:27.750 --> 00:11:28.960
warriors,

00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:30.837
with certainly a few budding
admirals and generals

00:11:30.837 --> 00:11:32.900
in the mix I'm sure.

00:11:32.900 --> 00:11:34.010
So,

00:11:34.010 --> 00:11:35.090
Muhammad,

00:11:35.090 --> 00:11:35.923
Valenti,

00:11:35.923 --> 00:11:37.860
Kay, Bella, Sophia,

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:39.650
Charlie and Jacob.

00:11:39.650 --> 00:11:40.840
You too Alegra.

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:42.430
In short order.

00:11:42.430 --> 00:11:44.860
Welcome to the officer's
mess and the wardroom.

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:46.245
Thank you.

00:11:46.245 --> 00:11:49.245
(audience clapping)

00:11:59.740 --> 00:12:00.573
- At this time,

00:12:00.573 --> 00:12:01.490
I'd like to welcome,

00:12:01.490 --> 00:12:02.770
Colonel Grandholm.

00:12:02.770 --> 00:12:06.083
Commander of Air force
ROTC at Yale university.

00:12:07.721 --> 00:12:10.721
(audience clapping)

00:12:13.550 --> 00:12:15.540
- Thank you, Cadet Rebelson.

00:12:17.840 --> 00:12:19.840
So good afternoon, ladies and gentlemen.

00:12:21.010 --> 00:12:24.410
Today, is indeed a special
day for our graduates.

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:27.120
Both for the journeys, that
they are about to take,

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:28.252
and for the obstacles,

00:12:28.252 --> 00:12:30.583
they have overcome over
the past four years.

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:33.730
I think we can all agree,

00:12:33.730 --> 00:12:36.100
that this year has been unlike any other,

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:38.293
in the history of ROTC at Yale.

00:12:39.730 --> 00:12:42.690
It's been a year of immense challenges,

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:45.743
and personal loss, for many
of us here in the audience.

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:50.060
We have lost, friends and family members.

00:12:50.060 --> 00:12:53.670
We have faced financial and
professional disruptions.

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:56.320
And we have struggled to
maintain personal connections,

00:12:56.320 --> 00:12:57.590
via zoom,

00:12:57.590 --> 00:13:00.590
while sequestered away from
each other, for over a year now.

00:13:01.850 --> 00:13:03.730
Yet with all that we have faced,

00:13:03.730 --> 00:13:06.010
I think, it is even more important,

00:13:06.010 --> 00:13:07.663
for us to come together today,

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:11.330
and celebrate this momentous occasion.

00:13:11.330 --> 00:13:14.930
As the members, of the ROTC Class of 21,

00:13:14.930 --> 00:13:17.450
affirm, their willingness
to support and defend

00:13:17.450 --> 00:13:19.583
our country as commissioned officers.

00:13:20.540 --> 00:13:23.220
I'd like to first acknowledge
the incredible support,

00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:25.220
that we receive from the Yale community.

00:13:26.200 --> 00:13:27.033
Demonstrated,

00:13:27.033 --> 00:13:29.330
by the presence of our
distinguished guests today.

00:13:30.470 --> 00:13:31.583
President Salovey,

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:34.640
thank you for your personal engagement

00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:36.313
on behalf of our cadets.

00:13:37.340 --> 00:13:38.330
Provost Strobel,

00:13:38.330 --> 00:13:39.700
Secretary Goff-Crews.

00:13:39.700 --> 00:13:41.280
and Chaplain Kugler.

00:13:41.280 --> 00:13:43.950
I greatly appreciate
your presence here today.

00:13:43.950 --> 00:13:45.823
And your support of our ROTC program.

00:13:47.300 --> 00:13:48.460
Dean Chun,

00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:50.006
thank you for helping our students

00:13:50.006 --> 00:13:52.930
through the many challenges
they have faced this year.

00:13:52.930 --> 00:13:56.570
And for expediting
numerous, special requests

00:13:56.570 --> 00:13:58.283
for essential military training.

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:03.210
Thank you to the deans
and heads of colleges,

00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:05.330
who have joined us today,

00:14:05.330 --> 00:14:09.310
for your patience in dealing
with arcane military paperwork,

00:14:09.310 --> 00:14:11.160
and for the personal care and concern,

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:13.043
that you have shown for your students.

00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:15.830
Thank you also, to Adrian,

00:14:15.830 --> 00:14:16.663
Henry,

00:14:16.663 --> 00:14:17.620
and Sean.

00:14:17.620 --> 00:14:19.770
Representing the Yale
Veterans Association,

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:21.790
and the Yale Veterans Network.

00:14:21.790 --> 00:14:24.080
For all the support you
have given our cadets.

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:25.433
Financial and otherwise.

00:14:26.570 --> 00:14:29.520
I'd also like to recognize
our ROTC liaisons.

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:31.610
George Lavesque and Risa Sodi.

00:14:31.610 --> 00:14:33.770
Who not only have been
forced to learn Air Force

00:14:33.770 --> 00:14:35.970
and Navy lingo and acronyms.

00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:39.610
But who also worked tirelessly
to advocate on our behalf,

00:14:39.610 --> 00:14:41.160
with the larger Yale community.

00:14:42.630 --> 00:14:44.760
Along with the air force and Navy cadre,

00:14:44.760 --> 00:14:46.760
who made the ceremony possible today.

00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:48.350
I'd like to especially highlight,

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:50.060
our two planning leads,

00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:53.120
Cadet Anne Northrup and
Cadet Molly Rockelset.

00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:56.250
I think we can all agree, that
they deserve the most credit

00:14:56.250 --> 00:14:58.590
of anyone, in making
this ceremony possible.

00:14:58.590 --> 00:15:00.245
Thank you for volunteering.

00:15:00.245 --> 00:15:03.245
(audience clapping)

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:11.250
And, to all of you, joining
us via the live stream,

00:15:11.250 --> 00:15:12.980
including the friends and families,

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:15.100
of our graduating cadets and midshipmen.

00:15:15.100 --> 00:15:15.933
Welcome.

00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:20.763
So to our Air Force ROTC Class of 2021.

00:15:21.890 --> 00:15:25.080
You are about to take the
next step on a journey,

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:27.480
shared by men and women in uniform,

00:15:27.480 --> 00:15:30.953
since the continental army,
was established in 1775.

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:33.600
An army, which included students,

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:35.940
who stood just steps away from here,

00:15:35.940 --> 00:15:38.690
on their commencement day
two and a half centuries ago.

00:15:41.230 --> 00:15:44.400
You have all heard me
say, probably a few times,

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:46.190
that ROTC is first and foremost,

00:15:46.190 --> 00:15:47.950
a leadership program.

00:15:47.950 --> 00:15:50.350
That, regardless of what
job you are going to do,

00:15:50.350 --> 00:15:52.350
in the Air Force or Space Force,

00:15:52.350 --> 00:15:55.440
you will all be leading,
our airmen and guardians,

00:15:55.440 --> 00:15:57.390
in accomplishing your assigned mission.

00:15:58.290 --> 00:16:00.300
Fortunately, for those
airmen and guardians,

00:16:00.300 --> 00:16:03.883
we have an incredible group
of leaders in the class of 21.

00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:08.530
Your leadership, is what
allowed our cadet wing,

00:16:08.530 --> 00:16:11.970
to overcome the countless
challenges we face this year.

00:16:11.970 --> 00:16:15.250
To create, an entirely new
hybrid training program,

00:16:15.250 --> 00:16:17.220
at a few weeks notice.

00:16:17.220 --> 00:16:19.270
And to do it all, with a positive attitude

00:16:19.270 --> 00:16:20.283
and can-do spirit.

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:23.930
You have innovated,

00:16:23.930 --> 00:16:27.410
you have raised the
intensity, when necessary,

00:16:27.410 --> 00:16:29.870
and you have shown, our
first and second year cadets,

00:16:29.870 --> 00:16:31.133
how to lead.

00:16:32.180 --> 00:16:34.460
But what I am most grateful for,

00:16:34.460 --> 00:16:36.072
is the care and concern,

00:16:36.072 --> 00:16:39.210
you have demonstrated
for your fellow cadets.

00:16:39.210 --> 00:16:42.020
You have internalized the
most important attribute,

00:16:42.020 --> 00:16:44.280
you will need as a military leader.

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:46.663
Putting the needs of the
team, above your own.

00:16:48.470 --> 00:16:50.040
Almost a 105 years ago,

00:16:50.040 --> 00:16:53.170
12 Yale students, founded
the Yale Aero club.

00:16:53.170 --> 00:16:56.430
Which later formed the nucleus
of the first deal unit.

00:16:56.430 --> 00:17:00.730
When they took their
oaths, on March 24 1917,

00:17:00.730 --> 00:17:03.330
at the Naval submarine base in New London.

00:17:03.330 --> 00:17:04.950
They probably could not imagine,

00:17:04.950 --> 00:17:05.790
that their service,

00:17:05.790 --> 00:17:07.760
would bring them to the
battlefields of Belgium,

00:17:07.760 --> 00:17:09.923
and France, in under a year.

00:17:11.240 --> 00:17:14.230
I know that when I was
commissioned in 1998.

00:17:14.230 --> 00:17:15.810
I would not have predicted, that a little

00:17:15.810 --> 00:17:17.290
over three years later,

00:17:17.290 --> 00:17:20.250
the United States would
embark on the longest war,

00:17:20.250 --> 00:17:21.770
in our history.

00:17:21.770 --> 00:17:24.660
A war, for which I, and most
of our cadre members here

00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:27.720
today, have deployed multiple times,

00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:29.670
as well as some of the veterans,

00:17:29.670 --> 00:17:31.463
with the YVA and YVN.

00:17:32.890 --> 00:17:34.310
For all of you about to join,

00:17:34.310 --> 00:17:36.093
our nations officer corps today.

00:17:37.130 --> 00:17:40.090
I do not know where
and how you will serve.

00:17:40.090 --> 00:17:42.973
Or if your service, will
lead you into combat.

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:45.560
What I do know,

00:17:45.560 --> 00:17:47.910
after spending the past year with you,

00:17:47.910 --> 00:17:50.330
is that I am confident
you will serve with honor

00:17:50.330 --> 00:17:51.203
and distinction.

00:17:52.340 --> 00:17:55.220
And will lead our air
force and space force,

00:17:55.220 --> 00:17:58.730
through whatever challenges
we face in the years to come.

00:17:58.730 --> 00:18:01.010
I congratulate each of you,

00:18:01.010 --> 00:18:01.850
and I thank you,

00:18:01.850 --> 00:18:04.763
for the spirit of service, on
behalf of our great nation.

00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:08.430
It is now my great pleasure to introduce,

00:18:08.430 --> 00:18:09.920
Dr. Peter Salovey,

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:12.490
23rd president of Yale University,

00:18:12.490 --> 00:18:15.320
and the Chris Argyris
Professor of Psychology.

00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:17.410
It is entirely fitting
that President Salovey,

00:18:17.410 --> 00:18:19.470
is our guest of honor today.

00:18:19.470 --> 00:18:21.490
His groundbreaking work,
with John D. Mayer,

00:18:21.490 --> 00:18:24.280
on emotional intelligence, is used widely,

00:18:24.280 --> 00:18:26.300
across the department of defense.

00:18:26.300 --> 00:18:27.990
And is now an integral part,

00:18:27.990 --> 00:18:30.790
of how the air force
trains our future leaders.

00:18:30.790 --> 00:18:32.760
Presidents Salovey, has
been a strong supporter

00:18:32.760 --> 00:18:35.500
of the ROTC program at Yale.

00:18:35.500 --> 00:18:36.840
And going back to his time,

00:18:36.840 --> 00:18:40.270
as an intern, at the west
Haven, VA medical center.

00:18:40.270 --> 00:18:43.650
He truly understands
issues, facing our veterans.

00:18:43.650 --> 00:18:45.660
And active duty military members.

00:18:45.660 --> 00:18:46.630
Ladies and gentlemen.

00:18:46.630 --> 00:18:47.463
Thank you.

00:18:47.463 --> 00:18:48.823
President Salovey.

00:18:48.823 --> 00:18:51.823
(audience clapping)

00:19:07.740 --> 00:19:09.093
- Good afternoon, everyone.

00:19:10.500 --> 00:19:13.950
It's truly an honor, to be
here to congratulate the class

00:19:13.950 --> 00:19:15.610
of 2021,

00:19:15.610 --> 00:19:18.073
of the Yale reserve
officers training corps.

00:19:18.950 --> 00:19:22.080
And thank you, Colonel
Granholm and Captain Withrow,

00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:24.210
for inviting me to do so.

00:19:24.210 --> 00:19:25.880
To all the families,

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:28.230
and friends, and loved ones,

00:19:28.230 --> 00:19:30.114
watching via live stream.

00:19:30.114 --> 00:19:32.023
You're very close to our hearts.

00:19:32.930 --> 00:19:34.210
On behalf of my wife,

00:19:34.210 --> 00:19:35.310
Marta,

00:19:35.310 --> 00:19:38.110
and my colleagues across the university.

00:19:38.110 --> 00:19:38.943
Thank you,

00:19:38.943 --> 00:19:40.480
for all you have done,

00:19:40.480 --> 00:19:42.750
to support these cadets,

00:19:42.750 --> 00:19:43.723
our graduates.

00:19:45.360 --> 00:19:48.610
And finally to the ROTC class of 2021.

00:19:48.610 --> 00:19:51.610
Thank you, for your commitment to Yale.

00:19:51.610 --> 00:19:53.520
To the United States.

00:19:53.520 --> 00:19:54.493
To the common good.

00:19:55.620 --> 00:19:58.743
We're proud of all you've
accomplished at this university.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:02.763
But even more, we are
grateful for your service.

00:20:03.780 --> 00:20:06.670
Soon, you will not only
graduate from Yale.

00:20:06.670 --> 00:20:09.270
But also receive your commissions
into the United States,

00:20:09.270 --> 00:20:10.403
armed forces.

00:20:11.260 --> 00:20:12.670
You will affirm your allegiance,

00:20:12.670 --> 00:20:15.440
to the constitution, of the
United States of America,

00:20:15.440 --> 00:20:18.163
and pledge to defend, our democracy.

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:23.510
You take on this responsibility
at a critical moment,

00:20:23.510 --> 00:20:24.833
in our nation's history.

00:20:26.320 --> 00:20:29.470
The COVID-19 pandemic,
continues to claim lives,

00:20:29.470 --> 00:20:30.513
around the world.

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:34.810
The geopolitical effects of
this crisis will be felt,

00:20:34.810 --> 00:20:35.993
for years to come.

00:20:37.250 --> 00:20:39.590
And we face other challenges.

00:20:39.590 --> 00:20:41.350
Extremism,

00:20:41.350 --> 00:20:43.470
sectarian violence,

00:20:43.470 --> 00:20:45.500
environmental catastrophe,

00:20:45.500 --> 00:20:47.020
poverty.

00:20:47.020 --> 00:20:51.330
And yet, in spite of these
and other serious threats,

00:20:51.330 --> 00:20:53.690
I'm encouraged by what I see.

00:20:53.690 --> 00:20:55.190
Talented,

00:20:55.190 --> 00:20:56.780
promising,

00:20:56.780 --> 00:20:58.423
courageous men and women.

00:20:59.580 --> 00:21:01.310
You've worked hard at Yale.

00:21:01.310 --> 00:21:02.640
In your classes,

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:04.093
in your ROTC training.

00:21:05.570 --> 00:21:08.870
You've made personal
sacrifices in the service,

00:21:08.870 --> 00:21:10.810
of a larger goal,

00:21:10.810 --> 00:21:14.193
to make our nation and our
world safer, and more secure.

00:21:15.610 --> 00:21:18.513
You have done this, not,
just for yourselves,

00:21:19.370 --> 00:21:20.963
in fact not for yourselves,

00:21:21.810 --> 00:21:22.793
but for others.

00:21:23.800 --> 00:21:25.710
Not only for the present,

00:21:25.710 --> 00:21:27.453
but for future generations.

00:21:28.750 --> 00:21:31.193
Although you have chosen a difficult path.

00:21:32.050 --> 00:21:34.053
You do not go at it alone.

00:21:34.950 --> 00:21:37.500
You join a great legacy
of military service

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:38.703
at this university.

00:21:39.670 --> 00:21:41.890
You go in the footsteps of Nathan Hale,

00:21:41.890 --> 00:21:45.270
Yale college class of 1773.

00:21:45.270 --> 00:21:47.023
And revolutionary war hero.

00:21:48.230 --> 00:21:50.500
And of Cortlandt van Rensselaer Creed.

00:21:50.500 --> 00:21:53.400
The first African-American,
to earn his medical degree

00:21:53.400 --> 00:21:54.570
from Yale.

00:21:54.570 --> 00:21:59.070
Creed, graduated at 1857
and joined the union army,

00:21:59.070 --> 00:22:01.223
as soon as he was permitted to enlist.

00:22:02.670 --> 00:22:06.040
You go in the footsteps of
Rear Admiral Grace Hopper.

00:22:06.040 --> 00:22:07.943
Who earned her PhD at Yale.

00:22:08.790 --> 00:22:10.930
When she retired from the Navy,

00:22:10.930 --> 00:22:13.210
at the age of 79.

00:22:13.210 --> 00:22:17.560
Amazing Grace, was the
oldest serving officer,

00:22:17.560 --> 00:22:19.763
in the U.S Armed Forces.

00:22:21.699 --> 00:22:22.590
And you tread the path,

00:22:22.590 --> 00:22:25.370
walked by many others, whose names are,

00:22:25.370 --> 00:22:26.913
not familiar to us.

00:22:27.990 --> 00:22:30.440
Nearly 30,000 Yale graduate served,

00:22:30.440 --> 00:22:32.940
in the first and second world wars.

00:22:32.940 --> 00:22:34.973
In Korea and Vietnam.

00:22:36.090 --> 00:22:37.930
Others have fought in recent wars,

00:22:37.930 --> 00:22:39.903
and serve around the globe today.

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:42.870
Your service honors those,

00:22:42.870 --> 00:22:45.730
who have made the ultimate sacrifice,

00:22:45.730 --> 00:22:47.493
in defense of this country.

00:22:49.200 --> 00:22:51.610
The names of over a thousand Yale alumni,

00:22:51.610 --> 00:22:54.060
killed in wars, since
the American revolution,

00:22:54.060 --> 00:22:57.790
are carved into the
walls of Memorial hall,

00:22:57.790 --> 00:22:58.653
right over their.

00:23:00.610 --> 00:23:02.810
The freedoms, we hold dear.

00:23:02.810 --> 00:23:05.270
The prosperity, we enjoy.

00:23:05.270 --> 00:23:07.110
Were made possible.

00:23:07.110 --> 00:23:10.883
By previous generations of
military, service, people.

00:23:12.400 --> 00:23:14.650
I know your decision,
to accept a commission

00:23:14.650 --> 00:23:18.350
in the United States armed
forces has not been made lightly.

00:23:18.350 --> 00:23:22.290
You've already given up, many
comforts and conveniences,

00:23:22.290 --> 00:23:25.423
and you will give up many
more, in the years ahead.

00:23:26.800 --> 00:23:27.850
Thank you.

00:23:27.850 --> 00:23:31.080
Thank you for showing us by your example,

00:23:31.080 --> 00:23:32.470
what it looks like,

00:23:32.470 --> 00:23:35.303
to make sacrifices, for the common good.

00:23:37.000 --> 00:23:40.320
Today, as we recognize your achievements.

00:23:40.320 --> 00:23:43.123
We also mark another important milestone.

00:23:44.030 --> 00:23:45.163
10 years ago,

00:23:46.330 --> 00:23:47.163
Yale College,

00:23:47.163 --> 00:23:48.004
voted,

00:23:48.004 --> 00:23:51.293
to restore the ROTC TL.

00:23:52.320 --> 00:23:53.993
I'm very proud of this decision.

00:23:54.870 --> 00:23:56.940
It is only right, that a university,

00:23:56.940 --> 00:23:59.080
with such a rich history of service,

00:23:59.080 --> 00:24:02.083
should welcome the ROTC on our campus.

00:24:03.260 --> 00:24:05.000
And over the past decade,

00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:06.930
we have worked with our military partners,

00:24:06.930 --> 00:24:10.450
to prioritize the Yale ROTC program,

00:24:10.450 --> 00:24:14.593
and expand support and
opportunities for veterans.

00:24:16.840 --> 00:24:19.940
Each cadet contributes to this community.

00:24:19.940 --> 00:24:23.231
You bring distinctive
perspectives and experiences,

00:24:23.231 --> 00:24:25.350
to our campus.

00:24:25.350 --> 00:24:26.830
You challenge and inspire,

00:24:26.830 --> 00:24:29.573
your fellow students, faculty, and staff.

00:24:30.540 --> 00:24:34.200
The ROTC presence brings
great honor to Yale.

00:24:34.200 --> 00:24:36.683
But its importance, is much broader.

00:24:37.840 --> 00:24:41.010
The partnership between
universities and the military,

00:24:41.010 --> 00:24:44.563
serves the country's,
long-term strategic interests.

00:24:46.050 --> 00:24:49.520
Both Yale and the U.S
military, prepare leaders,

00:24:49.520 --> 00:24:51.070
for the great challenges ahead.

00:24:52.260 --> 00:24:54.260
Yale will continue to advocate,

00:24:54.260 --> 00:24:55.530
for funding,

00:24:55.530 --> 00:24:56.943
and support for the ROTC.

00:24:58.040 --> 00:25:02.620
As we educate students for
careers, in every sector,

00:25:02.620 --> 00:25:03.903
including the military.

00:25:05.830 --> 00:25:08.250
Today, I joined with so many others,

00:25:08.250 --> 00:25:12.143
in applauding your
commitment, and your courage.

00:25:13.140 --> 00:25:17.660
You leave here, taking what
you have learned at Yale.

00:25:17.660 --> 00:25:21.500
And sharing it with a
world, in great need,

00:25:21.500 --> 00:25:23.543
of light and truth.

00:25:25.500 --> 00:25:26.673
Thank you all,

00:25:28.430 --> 00:25:30.300
for what you have done.

00:25:30.300 --> 00:25:31.133
Thank you all,

00:25:31.133 --> 00:25:32.790
for what you will do,

00:25:32.790 --> 00:25:34.940
to fulfill Yale's mission,

00:25:34.940 --> 00:25:37.300
to improve the world today,

00:25:37.300 --> 00:25:39.143
and for future generations.

00:25:40.060 --> 00:25:41.720
Congratulations,

00:25:41.720 --> 00:25:44.170
Yale ROTC class

00:25:44.170 --> 00:25:45.053
of 2021.

00:25:47.256 --> 00:25:50.256
(audience clapping)

00:25:57.880 --> 00:25:59.440
- Thank you, president Salovey.

00:25:59.440 --> 00:26:02.063
We sincerely appreciate,
you being with us today.

00:26:03.080 --> 00:26:05.150
Cadets, and Midshipman.

00:26:05.150 --> 00:26:07.183
Please take your places on the stage.

00:26:45.960 --> 00:26:47.340
Ladies and gentlemen,

00:26:47.340 --> 00:26:49.400
we have now come to the
part of the ceremony,

00:26:49.400 --> 00:26:51.140
for which these midshipmen and cadets,

00:26:51.140 --> 00:26:54.030
have worked for, over the past four years.

00:26:54.030 --> 00:26:55.670
The oath of office.

00:26:55.670 --> 00:26:57.260
Would everyone please rise,

00:26:57.260 --> 00:26:58.520
as the oath of office,

00:26:58.520 --> 00:27:00.610
is administered to those
being commissioned,

00:27:00.610 --> 00:27:02.960
as officers in the Marine Corps,

00:27:02.960 --> 00:27:03.960
Navy,

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:04.890
Air force

00:27:04.890 --> 00:27:06.093
and Space Force.

00:27:13.577 --> 00:27:16.577
(oaths being taken)

00:28:18.903 --> 00:28:21.903
(audience clapping)

00:28:38.990 --> 00:28:40.580
- Second Lieutenants and Ensigns,

00:28:40.580 --> 00:28:41.890
will pin on their new ranks,

00:28:41.890 --> 00:28:44.843
in private ceremonies,
with friends and families.

00:29:20.770 --> 00:29:23.270
Thank you all for
commemorating this occasion.

00:29:23.270 --> 00:29:24.143
Please be seated.

00:29:28.390 --> 00:29:29.490
Captain Withrow,

00:29:29.490 --> 00:29:30.600
Colonel Granholm,

00:29:30.600 --> 00:29:31.770
and President Salovey,

00:29:31.770 --> 00:29:33.790
will now present the
commissioning certificates,

00:29:33.790 --> 00:29:36.540
to the new ensigns and second lieutenants.

00:29:36.540 --> 00:29:37.780
Adrian Boninburgher.

